1. Foundational Definitions

1.1 State Space and Genome

Definition 1.1 (Biological state manifold).
Let 𝒳 be a smooth, compact, finite‑dimensional Riemannian manifold without boundary. Points x ∈ 𝒳 encode measurable biological states (e.g. transcriptomic vectors, morphological coordinates). Compactness ensures the existence of uniform covering numbers for Lipschitz function classes—a critical requirement for the convergence rate in §3.

Definition 1.2 (Genome).
A genome is a finite string G ∈ Σ^L, where Σ = {A, C, G, T} and L ≈ 3.2 × 10⁹ for humans. Σ^L carries the discrete σ‑algebra ℬ(Σ^L). Mutations are modelled as Markov kernels μ : Σ^L × ℬ(Σ^L) → [0,1].

1.2 Development as a Random Dynamical System

Development is a discrete‑time Markov process indexed by the genome and an environmental variable:

x_{t+1} \sim \mathbb{P}\!\big(\cdot \mid x_t,\, G,\, e_t\big),\qquad t = 0,1,\dots,T-1,
\tag{1}

where
 e_t ∈ ℰ is a stationary, bounded, ergodic environmental process,
 ℙ(·|x,G,e) is a transition kernel belonging to a class 𝒦 of measurable stochastic kernels on 𝒳.

We assume that for each fixed G the process is geometrically ergodic: there exists a unique invariant measure π_G on 𝒳 and the transition kernel mixes at exponential rate. This condition ensures the disintegration theorem yields a well‑defined regular conditional distribution of the operator given the genome (see §1.3) and guarantees that the data distribution has full support, a key ingredient for KL‑identifiability (§3.2).

1.3 Genomic Operators and the Measure‑Valued Extraction Map

Definition 1.3 (Genomic operator).
A genomic operator is a measurable self‑map P : 𝒳 → 𝒳. We restrict attention to a compact operator class:

Definition 1.4 (Compact operator class).
Let 𝒫 ⊂ C⁰(𝒳,𝒳) be the set of Lipschitz maps P : 𝒳 → 𝒳 with Lipschitz constant Lip(P) ≤ L, for some fixed L > 0. Endowed with the topology of uniform convergence, 𝒫 is compact by the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem. Compactness guarantees that Rademacher complexities of associated loss classes are finite and that FNO architectures can approximate it uniformly (§6).

Definition 1.5 (Genomic design pattern).
A design pattern is a probability measure μ ∈ 𝒫(𝒫)—the space of Borel probability measures on 𝒫 equipped with the weak topology—that simultaneously satisfies:

1. Reusability: the support of μ consists of operators that appear recurrently in the decomposition of ℙ at multiple developmental stages;
2. Evolutionary invariance: μ is a local maximum of the expected fitness functional 𝔉 under Wright–Fisher population dynamics (rigorously defined in §8);
3. Cross‑organism conservation: μ is recoverable from Φ(G′) for phylogenetically independent genomes G′ at a prescribed taxonomic resolution τ.

Definition 1.6 (Measure‑valued extraction operator).

\Phi : \Sigma^L \longrightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}),\qquad 
G \longmapsto \mu_G,
\tag{2}

where μ_G is the regular conditional distribution of the operator P given the genome G, obtained by disintegration of the joint distribution of (G, P) induced by the developmental system. This map is measurable from (Σ^L, ℬ(Σ^L)) to 𝒫(𝒫) with the evaluation σ‑algebra—a consequence of the Giry monad: for any measurable space (Ω, ℱ), the assignment Ω ↦ 𝒫(Ω) is the object part of a monad, and Φ is a Kleisli arrow for that monad. The disintegration theorem guarantees the existence of μ_G almost surely under the ergodicity assumption.

The shift from the earlier set‑valued Φ to a measure‑valued Φ is compulsory for any statistical inference theory: a probability structure on the pre‑image of G is required to define posterior consistency and convergence rates.

