2. The Ill‑Posedness of Exact Recovery

We formalise the fundamental limits that any extraction procedure must confront.

Definition 2.1 (Observational data).
Given N independent developmental trajectories of length T,

\mathcal{D} = \big\{\, (x_t^{(i)}, x_{t+1}^{(i)}) \,\big\}_{i=1, t=0}^{N, T-1}.
\tag{3}

Definition 2.2 (Observational equivalence).
Two operators P, Q ∈ 𝒫 are 𝒟‑equivalent, written P ∼_𝒟 Q, if P(x_t^{(i)}) = Q(x_t^{(i)}) for all (i,t) in 𝒟.

Theorem 2.1 (Topological ill‑posedness of Φ).
For the extraction map Φ defined in (2), the following hold generically.

(a) Non‑injectivity.
There exist distinct genomes G₁ ≠ G₂ such that Φ(G₁) = Φ(G₂).
Proof obligation. This follows from the well‑documented existence of neutral mutations. A formal bound on the size of the neutral space is provided by Dingle et al. (2025): the probability that a point mutation transforms a phenotype (here, an operator measure) from μ₁ to μ₂ is bounded above by 2^{−K(μ₂|μ₁)}, where K is conditional Kolmogorov complexity. Because K(μ|μ) is small, neutral spaces have positive measure under the mutation kernel. The theorem requires the construction of at least one explicit neutral mutation pair; the thesis will produce a concrete example using a gene regulatory network model whose attractor structure is invariant under a compensatory mutation.

(b) Discontinuity.
Φ is not continuous when Σ^L carries any non‑trivial metric and 𝒫(𝒫) carries the weak topology.
Proof obligation. The genome space is essentially discrete; any reasonable metric must assign distance zero to identical sequences and positive distance to distinct sequences. A single‑nucleotide change can alter a transcription factor binding site and cause a bifurcation in the developmental attractor landscape—the invariant measure π_G jumps, and consequently Φ(G) changes abruptly. The thesis will construct an explicit sequence G_n → G (say, in the Hamming metric) for which Φ(G_n) converges weakly to a measure distinct from Φ(G), using a mathematical model of the segment polarity network in Drosophila, where a single mutation in the engrailed locus can switch the system between a one‑attractor and a two‑attractor regime.

(c) Non‑identifiability from finite data.
For any finite data set 𝒟, the posterior over Φ(G) is not a Dirac mass; the equivalence class [P]_𝒟 is always infinite‑dimensional.
Proof. The support of the observed covariates {x_t^{(i)}} is a finite set of points in the manifold 𝒳, a set of Lebesgue measure zero. Any two Lipschitz operators that agree on this finite set but differ elsewhere are observationally equivalent yet distinct elements of 𝒫. Because 𝒫 is an infinite‑dimensional function space, the equivalence class contains an infinite‑dimensional subspace. Consequently, the likelihood cannot concentrate on a single operator, and the posterior remains non‑degenerate. This is a direct lifting of the standard nonparametric identifiability result in regression to the measure‑valued setting.

The theorem establishes that exact recovery is impossible, forcing a regularised statistical formulation.

