3. The Well‑Posed Bayesian Inverse Problem

We reframe extraction as a regularised minimum‑risk estimation on the space of operator measures.

3.1 Regularised Empirical Risk Formulation

Let 𝒫 be the compact operator class, and let ℳ_Θ = { μ_θ : θ ∈ Θ } be a parametrised family of probability measures on 𝒫 (e.g. pushforwards of a Gaussian measure by a neural network). Given a prior π ∈ 𝒫(𝒫) and a likelihood function q(·|·) (e.g. Gaussian with known variance), the regularised empirical risk estimator is

\hat{\mu}_{\mathcal{D}} = \arg\min_{\theta\in\Theta} \; 
\Big\{
-\frac{1}{NT}\sum_{i,t} \log \int_{\mathcal{P}} q\big(x_{t+1}^{(i)} \mid P(x_t^{(i)})\big) \, \mathrm{d}\mu_\theta(P)
\;+\; \lambda \cdot \mathrm{KL}(\mu_\theta \| \pi)
\Big\}.
\tag{4}

The inner integral is the evidence, the first term is the negative log‑likelihood, and the second term penalises deviation from the prior. This is a Gibbs posterior; it reduces to the standard Bayesian update when λ = 1 and the model is correctly specified.

3.2 KL‑Identifiability: The Core Assumption

For the estimator to be consistent, the model family must be identifiable in the following sense.

Definition 3.1 (KL‑identifiability).
The family { μ_θ : θ ∈ Θ } with likelihood q is KL‑identifiable if for any θ₁, θ₂,

\mathrm{KL}\big( p(\mathcal{D}\mid\mu_{\theta_1}) \,\|\, p(\mathcal{D}\mid\mu_{\theta_2}) \big) = 0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \mu_{\theta_1} = \mu_{\theta_2},
\tag{5}

where p(𝒟|μ_θ) is the marginal likelihood of the observed transitions under the operator measure μ_θ.

This condition is the minimum requirement for posterior consistency in the sense of Schwartz (1965): if the prior assigns positive mass to every KL‑neighborhood of the true measure, the posterior concentrates weakly at the truth. More recent work by Bariletto, Flores & Walker (2025) shows that sequential identifiability alone suffices, but KL‑identifiability is the cleanest route and is standard in nonparametric Bayes (Ghosal & van der Vaart, 2017).

Verification for the Gaussian–Lipschitz model (a thesis obligation).
For q(x_{t+1}|P(x_t)) = 𝒩(x_{t+1}; P(x_t), σ²I), two likelihoods have zero KL divergence if and only if P₁(x) = P₂(x) for almost all x under the data distribution. Because the developmental process is ergodic and the manifold is compact, the empirical distribution of x_t converges to an invariant measure with full support on an open subset of 𝒳. For Lipschitz operators, equality a.e. on an open set implies equality everywhere. Hence, μ₁ = μ₂ as measures on 𝒫 is equivalent to the equality of the pushforward distributions of the operator under the data; this establishes KL‑identifiability. The thesis will present the detailed measure‑theoretic argument.

3.3 Convergence Rate Theorem

Theorem 3.2 (Convergence rate of the regularised estimator).
Assume:

1. 𝒫 is the compact class of Lipschitz maps with constant ≤ L.
2. The transition kernel ℙ is geometrically ergodic; the environmental process e_t is bounded and stationary.
3. The family {μ_θ} satisfies KL‑identifiability (Definition 3.1).
4. The prior π has full support on 𝒫(𝒫) and satisfies a log‑Sobolev inequality.

Then, as N, T → ∞, the minimiser μ̂ = μ_{θ̂} from (4) converges to the true operator measure μ* in the 2‑Wasserstein metric W₂ at rate

\mathbb{E}\big[ W_2(\hat{\mu}, \mu^*) \big] \;=\; O\!\Big( (NT)^{-\frac{1}{d+2}} + \lambda \Big),
\tag{6}

where d is the intrinsic dimension of the operator class 𝒫 (roughly, its metric entropy).

Proof structure (4‑step scaffold).

Step 1 – Uniform empirical process bound.
The log‑likelihood ℓ(P; x, y) = −log q(y|P(x)) is Lipschitz in P with constant O(1/σ²) under the Gaussian likelihood. Over the compact class 𝒫, the Rademacher complexity of the induced function class ℱ = { ℓ(P; ·,·) : P ∈ 𝒫 } scales as O((NT)^{−1/2} × N(ε, 𝒫)^{1/2}), where N(ε, 𝒫) is the covering number at scale ε. For Lipschitz functions on a d‑dimensional manifold, log N(ε, 𝒫) ≍ ε^{−d}. Optimising ε yields the uniform deviation bound

\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}} \Big| \frac{1}{NT}\sum \ell(P) - \mathbb{E}\ell(P) \Big| = O_{\mathbb{P}}\big( (NT)^{-\frac{1}{d+2}} \big).
\tag{7}

This uses classical empirical process theory (van der Vaart & Wellner, 1996).

Step 2 – Uniqueness of the population minimiser.
KL‑identifiability guarantees that the expected loss 𝔼[ℓ(P)] has a unique minimiser at the true operator P* (or at the true measure μ). The M‑estimation argument then transfers the uniform convergence to a bound on the distance between the empirical minimiser μ̂ and μ.

Step 3 – Regularisation bias.
The KL penalty term constrains the estimator to a Wasserstein ball around the prior. The radius is O(λ^{1/2}) if the prior has a log‑Sobolev constant. Setting λ ≍ (NT)^{−1/(d+2)} balances the bias from regularisation with the variance from the empirical process.

Step 4 – From KL to Wasserstein via Talagrand’s inequality.
If the prior π and the true posterior satisfy a log‑Sobolev inequality (true for many standard priors on compact spaces), Talagrand’s T₂ inequality gives W₂²(μ₁, μ₂) ≤ C·KL(μ₁‖μ₂). Applying this to the empirical risk bound yields the stated rate. In the non‑dominated case where the posterior is not absolutely continuous with respect to the prior (e.g. when λ is small), we employ the Wasserstein posterior contraction framework of Dolera, Mainini et al. (2020–2022), which replaces Talagrand’s inequality with a sieve‑based argument on 𝒫(𝒫).

The rate (NT)^{−1/(d+2)} depends on the intrinsic dimension d of 𝒫. If 𝒫 is parametrised by a neural operator with m parameters, d = m and the rate is (NT)^{−1/(m+2)}—fast for moderate m, but subject to the curse of dimensionality. This is the price paid for escaping the infinite‑dimensional ill‑posedness of Theorem 2.1.

