4. Operator Algebra and Markov‑Category Structure

The extracted measures are not isolated objects; they carry algebraic and categorical structure that reflects evolutionary processes.

4.1 The Topological Monoid of Operators

On the compact class 𝒫, composition ∘ is defined pointwise. If the Lipschitz constant L ≤ 1, then Lip(P∘Q) ≤ L² ≤ L, and 𝒫 is closed under composition; it then forms a topological monoid (𝒫, ∘, Id). If L > 1, we explicitly restrict to a subclass closed under composition. The fixed‑point sets Fix(P) = {x | P(x) = x} are compact and their topological features (e.g. Betti numbers via persistent homology) provide operator invariants that can be estimated from trajectory data.

4.2 Markov‑Category Enrichment: Φ as a Functor

Definition 4.1 (Markov category Gen).
Objects are equivalence classes of genomes under neutral mutation. A morphism G₁ → G₂ is a stochastic kernel f : Σ^L × ℬ(Σ^L) → [0,1] representing the probability of mutating from any sequence in the equivalence class of G₁ to one in the class of G₂. Composition is Chapman–Kolmogorov composition; the category is semicartesian symmetric monoidal, hence a Markov category in the sense of Fritz (2020).

Definition 4.2 (Markov category OpProb).
Objects are probability spaces (𝒫, μ) with μ ∈ 𝒫(𝒫). A morphism (𝒫, μ) → (𝒫, ν) is a stochastic kernel κ : 𝒫 × ℬ(𝒫) → [0,1] that is equivariant with respect to the composition monoid action: κ(P∘Q, ·) = κ(P,·) ∘ Q in distribution.

Proposition 4.3 (Φ as a Markov functor).
There exists a functor Φ : Gen → OpProb that maps each genome class G to the operator measure Φ(G) = μ_G, and each stochastic mutation kernel f to a morphism in OpProb given by the pushforward of μ_G under the mutation‑induced transformation of the operator.
Proof obligation. The construction uses the disintegration theorem: given the joint law of (G₁, P) and a mutation kernel f from G₁ to G₂, the induced operator measure for G₂ is obtained by integrating the conditional operator measure over the mutation distribution. The equivariance condition holds exactly when mutations have small effect—the operator changes linearly in distribution. The thesis will verify this condition in a perturbative regime and derive an explicit Wasserstein error bound for large‑effect mutations.

This functor provides a rigorous language for stating results about evolutionary information transfer: e.g. the Blackwell–Sherman–Stein theorem on comparison of statistical experiments can be expressed in OpProb, allowing one to quantify when one genome’s operator measure is “more informative” than another’s. This is a concrete application of the categorical machinery to biological information processing.

