5. Drift Estimation and Score‑Based Operator Extraction

5.1 Equivalence between Operator Extraction and Drift Estimation

In the continuous‑time limit, the Markov kernel ℙ(·|x,G,e) can be approximated by a stochastic differential equation

\mathrm{d}x_t = b(x_t, G)\,\mathrm{d}t + \sigma\,\mathrm{d}W_t,
\tag{8}

where the drift b : 𝒳 → T𝒳 is a vector field. The Euler–Maruyama discretisation gives the one‑step operator

P(x) = x + b(x)\Delta t + o(\Delta t).

Thus, estimating the operator P from transition pairs is equivalent to estimating the drift b from discrete observations of a diffusion.

A 2024 result on inversions of stochastic processes from ergodic measures (cited in the background document) demonstrates that the drift b is not identifiable from the stationary measure π_G alone; it is identifiable from transition data under the stationary Fokker–Planck equation

\nabla\cdot(b\,\pi_G) = \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta\pi_G,
\tag{9}

provided the diffusion coefficient σ is known and the process is reversible. This theorem reinforces the necessity of trajectory data 𝒟 (transition pairs) rather than steady‑state samples.

5.2 Score‑Matching and the Drift Operator

Recent work on score‑based generative models provides a scalable drift estimator. Let S_φ(x, t, G) be a neural network trained via denoising score matching to approximate the score ∇_x log p_t(x|G). The reverse‑time SDE

\mathrm{d}x_t = \big[ b(x_t) - \sigma^2 S_\phi(x_t, T-t, G) \big] \mathrm{d}t + \sigma\,\mathrm{d}\overline{W}_t

recovers the forward drift via b(x) = lim_{Δt→0} (𝔼[x_{t+Δt}|x_t=x] − x)/Δt. The approximation error is bounded by the Fokker–Planck residual:

W_2(\hat{P}, P^*) \;\leq\; C \, \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{FP}}(S_\phi),
\tag{10}

where ℛ_FP is the Fokker–Planck equation residual integrated over the data distribution, and the constant C depends on the log‑Sobolev constant of the diffusion. This result, derived from the analysis of Song et al. (2021) and the Wasserstein bounds in Chen et al. (2022), provides a rigorous performance guarantee for the operator extracted via score matching. The thesis will formalise this bound and show how the diffusion‑based operator can be fed into the Bayesian framework of §3 as a proposal distribution.

