6. Neural Operator Architectures and Amortised Inference

6.1 Fourier Neural Operator as Universal Approximator

The computational backbone for representing operators is the Fourier Neural Operator (FNO). An FNO is a composition

P_\theta = \mathcal{Q} \circ \mathcal{L}_L \circ \cdots \circ \mathcal{L}_1 \circ \mathcal{R},
\qquad 
\mathcal{L}_k(v) = \sigma\!\big( W_k v + \mathcal{F}^{-1}(R_k \cdot \mathcal{F}(v)) \big),
\tag{11}

where ℱ is a discrete Fourier transform on a grid approximation of 𝒳. Kovachki et al. (2021) proved that FNOs are universal: for any continuous operator P ∈ C⁰(𝒳,𝒳) and ε > 0, there exists an FNO P_θ with O(ε^{−d} log(1/ε)) Fourier modes that approximates P uniformly on compact sets. Lanthaler, Li & Stuart (2024) sharpened this, showing that universality requires only non‑locality and non‑linearity, uniting FNOs with other architectures. Because our operator class 𝒫 is compact in the C⁰ topology, the FNO can approximate any P ∈ 𝒫 arbitrarily well. The thesis will quantify this approximation error in Wasserstein distance for the induced pushforward measure, a necessary step for the convergence of the amortised estimator.

6.2 Amortised Neural Bayes Estimator

Direct optimisation of (4) for each new genome is computationally prohibitive. We therefore train a neural Bayes estimator

\Psi_\varphi : \Sigma^L \longrightarrow \Theta,
\tag{12}

that maps a genome to the parameters θ of an operator measure μ_θ (e.g. the weights of a pre‑trained FNO). The genome is processed by a dilated convolutional network followed by transformer blocks (inspired by Evo 2) to produce a compact embedding, which is then projected to the parameter space.

Training objective. The amortised empirical risk is

\varphi^* = \arg\min_\varphi \mathbb{E}_{G\sim p(G),\,\mathcal{D}\sim p(\cdot|G)}\!\Big[
-\frac{1}{NT}\sum_{i,t} \log \int_{\mathcal{P}} q\big(x_{t+1}^{(i)} \mid P(x_t^{(i)})\big) \, \mathrm{d}\mu_{\Psi_\varphi(G)}(P)
+ \lambda \, \mathrm{KL}\big( \mu_{\Psi_\varphi(G)} \,\|\, \pi \Big)
\Big].
\tag{13}

Convergence guarantee.
The universality of the FNO ensures that the parametric family { μ_θ } is dense in 𝒫(𝒫) in the weak topology. The embedding network Ψ_φ is Lipschitz with respect to the genome metric (e.g. Hamming distance with transformer‑based smoothness). Under these conditions, a functional uniform law of large numbers guarantees that the amortised objective converges uniformly over G in the support of p(G). Consequently, the resulting measure μ_{Ψ_φ(G)} converges to the true posterior Φ(G) in W₂ as the training data and network capacity grow. The thesis will provide a rigorous statement and proof of this amortisation gap.

