7. Conditional Operator Learning for Perturbation Biology

To handle genetic or environmental perturbations, we extend the operator to a conditional form:

P(x, u) = P_{\text{base}}(x) + \Delta P(x, u),
\tag{14}

where u ∈ ℝ^m encodes the perturbation (e.g. a one‑hot gene‑knockout vector). The residual ΔP is a small adapter network that modulates the Fourier layers of the base operator. The architecture enforces ΔP(x, 0) = 0, so that the unperturbed case recovers the base developmental programme. Training on paired (control, perturbed) trajectory data yields a family of conditional operators that can be tested for cross‑lineage invariance—if the response operator ΔP(·, u) is conserved across genomes at a given τ, it is a design pattern in its own right and is added to the measure μ.

This module links the operator extraction to functional genomics, providing a concrete empirical validation path even though no laboratory experiments are performed in the PhD.

