8. Fitness Functional and Evolutionary Stability of Operator Measures

The definition of a design pattern (Def. 1.5(ii)) requires that μ is a local maximum of expected fitness. This must be translated into a formal statement.

Wright–Fisher operator replicator dynamics.
Let the population consist of a distribution of genomes G with frequencies f(G). Each genome induces an operator measure μ_G = Φ(G). Define the fitness of a genome as w(G) = 𝔼_{x∼π_G}[ φ(x) ], where φ is a phenotype‑to‑fitness function. The mean fitness of the population is w̄ = Σ_G f(G)w(G). Under the Wright–Fisher model with selection and mutation, the frequency dynamics in the infinite‑population limit is

\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} f(G) = f(G)\big[ w(G) - \overline{w} \big] + \sum_{G'} \big[ f(G')\, \mu(G' \to G) - f(G)\, \mu(G \to G') \big].
\tag{15}

By projecting this dynamic onto the space of operator measures via Φ, we obtain a replicator equation on 𝒫(𝒫). Let ν be a probability measure on 𝒫(𝒫) representing the current distribution of operator measures in the population. The dynamics is

\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \nu = \nu \big[ \mathbb{E}_{\mu\sim\nu}[\mathfrak{F}(\mu)] - \mathfrak{F}(\cdot) \big] + \text{mutation operator},
\tag{16}

where 𝔉(μ) = ∫ w(G) dμ_G^{-1}(μ) is the fitness of the operator measure μ (with the inverse image of Φ).

Evolutionary stability condition.
A design pattern μ* is a stable fixed point of (16) if:

1. 𝔉(μ*) is a strict local maximum in the space of operator measures;
2. The linearised dynamic at μ* has eigenvalues with negative real parts (Lyapunov stability).

The thesis will derive the linearisation explicitly: the Jacobian of the fitness gradient with respect to Wasserstein perturbations of μ is obtained via the Otto calculus on the Wasserstein space 𝒫(𝒫). The condition reduces to verifying that the second variational derivative δ²𝔉/δμ² is negative definite on the tangent cone. This is the most original mathematical component of the thesis and connects evolutionary game theory to optimal transport geometry.

