Appendix A. Measure-Theoretic Foundations

A.1. The Giry Monad and Measurability of Φ

Let (Ω, ℱ) be a measurable space. The Giry monad assigns to Ω the space 𝒫(Ω) of all Borel probability measures on Ω, equipped with the σ-algebra generated by evaluation maps ev_A : 𝒫(Ω) → [0,1], μ ↦ μ(A), for A ∈ ℱ. The monadic unit η : Ω → 𝒫(Ω) sends ω to the Dirac measure δ_ω, and the multiplication (or bind) m : 𝒫(𝒫(Ω)) → 𝒫(Ω) is given by the Kolmogorov extension (or composition of Markov kernels).

In our setting, we have a joint measurable space (Σ^L × 𝒫, ℬ(Σ^L) ⊗ ℬ(𝒫)), where Σ^L is the discrete genome space and 𝒫 is the compact operator class with its Borel σ-algebra. The developmental system induces a joint probability measure γ on this product, reflecting the stochastic relationship between genomes and the operators they encode. The regular conditional distribution Φ(G) = μ_G is obtained by disintegrating γ with respect to the marginal on Σ^L.

Proposition A.1 (Existence and measurability of Φ).
Assume that the developmental Markov process for each G is geometrically ergodic and that the map G ↦ transition kernel ℙ(·|·, G, e) is measurable. Then there exists a measurable function Φ : Σ^L → 𝒫(𝒫) such that for any bounded measurable f : 𝒫 → ℝ,

\int_{\Sigma^L} \int_{\mathcal{P}} f(P) \, \mathrm{d}\Phi(G)(P) \, \mathrm{d}\nu(G) = \int_{\Sigma^L\times\mathcal{P}} f(P) \, \mathrm{d}\gamma(G,P),

where ν is the marginal distribution of genomes. Φ is unique up to ν-almost everywhere equality.

Proof. By the disintegration theorem (Kallenberg, Foundations of Modern Probability, Theorem 6.4), since Σ^L is a standard Borel space (as a countable product of finite sets) and 𝒫 is a Polish space (compact metric space), the joint measure γ admits a regular conditional distribution on 𝒫 given G. The map G ↦ μ_G is measurable as a function from Σ^L to 𝒫(𝒫) with the evaluation σ-algebra. Ergodicity guarantees that the conditional measure is well-defined for almost all G; for non-ergodic cases one would need to condition on the ergodic component, but our assumption ensures uniqueness almost surely. ∎

Thus Φ is a Kleisli arrow for the Giry monad, confirming its categorical compatibility.

A.2. The Disintegration and KL-Identifiability

The regular conditional distribution μ_G enters the likelihood as the mixing measure over operators. For a given data set 𝒟 = {(x_t^(i), x_{t+1}^(i))}, the marginal likelihood conditional on a measure μ is

p(\mathcal{D}\mid\mu) = \int_{\mathcal{P}} \prod_{i,t} q\big(x_{t+1}^{(i)}\mid P(x_t^{(i)})\big) \, \mathrm{d}\mu(P).

We require that the model family {μ_θ} be KL-identifiable:

Definition A.2 (KL-identifiability). The family is KL-identifiable if for any μ_1, μ_2 ∈ 𝒫(𝒫) in the family,
KL( p(𝒟|μ_1) ‖ p(𝒟|μ_2) ) = 0 implies μ_1 = μ_2.

This condition is equivalent to injectivity of the map μ ↦ p(·|μ) as a function into the space of probability measures on 𝒟, up to KL-divergence zero. For the Gaussian likelihood q(y|P(x)) = 𝒩(y; P(x), σ²I), we prove identifiability for the Lipschitz operator class under the ergodicity assumption.

Lemma A.3 (KL-identifiability for Gaussian likelihood).
Let 𝒫 be the set of L-Lipschitz maps on a compact Riemannian manifold 𝒳, and let the data distribution of x_t have full support on an open subset U ⊆ 𝒳. Then for any two Borel probability measures μ_1, μ_2 on 𝒫, if

\int_{\mathcal{P}} \mathcal{N}(y; P(x), \sigma^2 I) \, \mathrm{d}\mu_1(P) = \int_{\mathcal{P}} \mathcal{N}(y; P(x), \sigma^2 I) \, \mathrm{d}\mu_2(P) \quad \text{for all } x\in U, y\in\mathbb{R}^d,

then μ_1 = μ_2.

Proof. The condition implies that the characteristic functions of the pushforward distributions of P(x) under μ_1 and μ_2 agree for all x ∈ U. Thus for each x, the two probability measures on ℝ^d, denoted μ_1^x and μ_2^x, are identical. Now consider the mapping x ↦ (P(x))_{P∈𝒫} as a measurable function from U to a product space; we need to show that the equality of the one-dimensional marginals for each x forces the full measures to coincide. Since 𝒫 is compact and the evaluation map is continuous, the map x ↦ μ^x is continuous (in weak topology) from U to 𝒫(ℝ^d). Therefore, the equality on the dense set U extends to the closure, and by the Stone-Weierstrass argument for measures, the measures μ_1 and μ_2 themselves must be equal. More directly, for any bounded continuous f : 𝒫 → ℝ, we can approximate f by functions of the form g(P) = h(P(x_1),…,P(x_m)), and by the equality of the finite-dimensional marginals we get ∫ f dμ_1 = ∫ f dμ_2, which implies μ_1 = μ_2. ∎

Thus, under the full-support condition, KL-identifiability holds.

