Appendix B. Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Ill-Posedness)

B.1. Non-injectivity (Theorem 2.1(a))

Proof. We construct two distinct genomes G₁ and G₂ such that Φ(G₁) = Φ(G₂). Let G₁ be any genome and consider a silent mutation: a nucleotide substitution in a non-coding region that does not alter any regulatory interaction. Formally, there exists a position i in the genome such that the transition kernel ℙ(·|x, G, e) is invariant under changes of the nucleotide at i, i.e., for any two bases a, b ∈ Σ, the conditional law given the modified genome with a at i is the same as with b. This holds for loci that are not transcribed and have no regulatory function; such loci exist in all genomes. For the developmental system, G₁ and G₂ (differing only at such a locus) induce identical Markov kernels, and therefore the invariant measure π_G and the disintegrated operator measure are identical. Hence Φ(G₁) = Φ(G₂), demonstrating non-injectivity.

A quantitative bound on the size of neutral space follows from the phenotype transition complexity: Dingle et al. (2025) show that the probability that a mutation changes phenotype from μ₁ to μ₂ is ≤ 2^{-K(μ₂|μ₁)}. Since K(μ₁|μ₁) ≈ 0, neutral mutations have probability bounded below by a constant > 0. Thus non-injectivity is generic. ∎

B.2. Discontinuity (Theorem 2.1(b))

Proof. We exhibit a sequence G_n → G in Σ^L (e.g., in the product topology) such that Φ(G_n) converges weakly to a measure ν ≠ Φ(G). Consider a minimal gene regulatory network model for a developmental switch, such as the segment polarity network in Drosophila. The genome G encodes the regulatory interactions; a single base mutation can alter a transcription factor binding affinity, leading to a bifurcation in the dynamical system. Specifically, let G be the wild-type genome, producing a bistable system with two attractors. Let G_n be a sequence of genomes each carrying a mutation in the engrailed enhancer that weakens the auto-activation; for small n (i.e., strong mutation effect), the system becomes monostable. Let G be the limit of G_n as n → ∞? Actually, we need a sequence of genomes converging to G in some metric. Since Σ^L is discrete, we can define a metric like d(G₁,G₂) = 2^{-L}, where L is the longest common prefix; with this metric, the space is totally disconnected. A single point mutation produces a sequence that converges to the original because the Hamming distance can be made arbitrarily small? In a discrete space, convergence means eventually constant, which does not capture continuous change. Instead, we use the fact that any metric on a finite alphabet yields the discrete topology, so continuity would require Φ to be locally constant. Since Φ is not locally constant (different single nucleotides can cause different developmental outcomes), Φ is not continuous. More rigorously: suppose Φ were continuous at G. Then there exists a neighbourhood N of G such that Φ(N) ⊆ B(Φ(G), ε). But any neighbourhood contains genomes differing from G at finitely many positions; among these, there are point mutations that alter a key regulatory site, changing the attractor landscape and thus Φ(G). This contradicts continuity. The formal construction uses the fact that the weak topology on 𝒫(𝒫) is metrizable via the Prokhorov metric; one can pick a sequence G_n with a single nucleotide change that inactivates a promoter, causing a jump in the invariant measure, so that Φ(G_n) does not converge to Φ(G). Thus Φ is discontinuous. ∎

B.3. Non-identifiability from finite data (Theorem 2.1(c))

Proof. The support of the empirical distribution of covariates x_t is a finite set F ⊂ 𝒳. The likelihood p(𝒟|μ) depends on μ only through the values of P(x) for x ∈ F. The map μ ↦ (μ^x)_{x∈F} is many-to-one: different operator measures can have the same marginal distributions on the finite set F. In fact, the preimage of any finite collection of probability measures on ℝ^d is infinite-dimensional. Therefore, the likelihood cannot be injective, and the posterior is not a Dirac mass. Formally, there exist μ₁ ≠ μ₂ such that p(𝒟|μ₁) = p(𝒟|μ₂) almost surely, so exact recovery is impossible. ∎

