Appendix D. Operator Algebra and Markov-Category Functor

D.1. Topological Monoid Structure

Let 𝒫 be the set of L-Lipschitz self-maps of 𝒳 with L ≤ 1. Then composition P ∘ Q is also L-Lipschitz (since Lip(P∘Q) ≤ Lip(P)·Lip(Q) ≤ L² ≤ L if L≤1). Thus 𝒫 is closed under composition. The identity map Id_𝒳 is 1-Lipschitz and belongs to 𝒫. Composition is associative and continuous in the C⁰ topology. Hence (𝒫, ∘, Id) is a topological monoid.

D.2. Markov Category Gen

Objects: equivalence classes of genomes under the neutral-mutation equivalence relation ~ defined by G₁ ~ G₂ if Φ(G₁) = Φ(G₂). For simplicity, we may take the objects as actual genomes with the understanding that morphisms are defined on the quotients.

Morphisms: a morphism G₁ → G₂ is a Markov kernel f : Σ^L × ℬ(Σ^L) → [0,1] representing the probability that a mutation (or sequence of mutations) converts a genome from class G₁ to a genome in class G₂. Composition is the Chapman–Kolmogorov composition. The category is semicartesian symmetric monoidal: the tensor product is concatenation of independent genomes, with the trivial morphism given by projection.

D.3. Markov Category OpProb

Objects: pairs (𝒫, μ) where μ ∈ 𝒫(𝒫). Morphisms: a morphism from (𝒫, μ) to (𝒫, ν) is a Markov kernel κ : 𝒫 × ℬ(𝒫) → [0,1] such that for all P ∈ 𝒫 and B ∈ ℬ(𝒫),

\int_{\mathcal{P}} \kappa(P\circ Q, B) \, d\mu(Q) = \int_{\mathcal{P}} \kappa(Q, B) \, d(\mu \circ P^{-1})(Q) \quad \text{(equivariance under right composition)}.

This ensures that composition with a fixed operator transforms the kernel appropriately. Composition of morphisms is the usual kernel composition.

D.4. Φ as a Functor

Define the functor Φ : Gen → OpProb as follows:

· On objects: Φ(G) = μ_G as defined.
· On morphisms: For a mutation kernel f : G₁ → G₂, define Φ(f) : (𝒫, μ_{G₁}) → (𝒫, μ_{G₂}) as the Markov kernel given by

\Phi(f)(P, B) = \int_{\Sigma^L} \mathbb{P}(G_2 \in dG \mid G_1, f) \, \delta_{\Phi(G)}(B),

where δ_{\Phi(G)} is the Dirac measure at the operator measure Φ(G) (viewed as a random element of 𝒫(𝒫)), and the integral is taken over the distribution of the resulting genome G₂ after mutation. In other words, we pushforward the operator measure by the stochastic mutation map.

To verify the functor axioms: identity morphism maps to the identity kernel (Dirac at the same operator measure). Composition follows from the Chapman–Kolmogorov property of mutation kernels and the linearity of pushforwards. The equivariance condition in OpProb morphisms must be checked. Under the assumption of small-effect mutations, the operator changes linearly: if G mutates to G′ with kernel f, then the induced operator measure μ_{G′} is approximately the pushforward of μ_G through a linear perturbation. For large-effect mutations, an error bound can be given; the category can be enriched with a metric to account for the approximation. For the PhD, we prove the functor property in the small-mutation regime.

