Appendix E. Drift Estimation and Score-Based Bound

E.1. Equivalence of Operator and Drift

In the continuous limit, the discrete map x_{t+1} ∼ ℙ(·|x_t) converges to an SDE dx_t = b(x_t) dt + σ dW_t. The Euler-Maruyama discretization gives the one-step conditional mean:

\mathbb{E}[x_{t+1} \mid x_t = x] = x + b(x) \Delta t + \mathcal{O}(\Delta t^2).

Therefore, the operator P(x) = x + b(x)Δt is a first-order approximation of the true drift. The error in operator recovery from drift estimation is of order Δt.

E.2. Identifiability from Transition Data

The stationary Fokker-Planck equation

\nabla\cdot(b \pi_G) = \frac{\sigma^2}{2} \Delta \pi_G

determines b up to a gradient of a harmonic function when the process is reversible. With full trajectory data (transition pairs), the drift is identifiable because the likelihood uses the transition density, which is uniquely determined by b and σ. The 2024 result mentioned shows that b is not identifiable from π_G alone, reinforcing the need for 𝒟.

E.3. Score Matching and Fokker-Planck Residual Bound

Let s_θ(x,t) be a score network trained to approximate ∇_x log p_t(x|G). The reverse-time SDE is

\mathrm{d}x_t = [b(x_t) - \sigma^2 s_\theta(x_t, T-t)] \mathrm{d}t + \sigma \mathrm{d}\overline{W}_t.

If s_θ perfectly matches the score of the forward process, the reverse process reproduces the correct trajectories. The approximation error is governed by the Fokker-Planck residual

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{FP}}(s_\theta) = \partial_t p_t + \nabla\cdot\big( (b - \sigma^2 s_\theta) p_t \big) - \frac{\sigma^2}{2} \Delta p_t.

Using results from Chen et al. (2022), one can bound the W₂ distance between the induced distribution of the approximate reverse process and the true forward process by C \cdot \sqrt{ \int_0^T \|\mathcal{R}{\mathrm{FP}}(s\theta)\|_{L^2(p_t)} \mathrm{d}t }. Thus, the operator estimated from the score network has a Wasserstein error bounded by the residual. This provides a rigorous error guarantee for the score-based operator extraction.

