Appendix F. Universal Approximation and Amortisation Gap

F.1. FNO Universality

Theorem F.1 (Kovachki et al., 2021; Lanthaler et al., 2024).
Let 𝒳 be a compact subset of ℝ^d, and let 𝒫 be the set of continuous operators from 𝒳 to 𝒳. For any ε > 0 and P ∈ 𝒫, there exists a Fourier neural operator P_θ with a finite number of Fourier layers such that

\sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \| P(x) - P_\theta(x) \| \le \varepsilon.

Moreover, the number of required Fourier modes scales as O(ε^{-d} \log(1/ε)). Lanthaler et al. show that non-locality and non-linearity are sufficient for universality, so other architectures also suffice.

Since our 𝒫 is a compact subset of C⁰(𝒳,𝒳), FNOs can approximate any operator in 𝒫 uniformly. This implies that pushforward measures induced by FNO parameters are dense in the weak topology on 𝒫(𝒫) (by continuous mapping).

F.2. Amortisation Convergence

Let μ_θ = (P_θ)#π be the operator measure obtained by pushing forward a fixed base measure π (e.g., a Gaussian over the FNO weight space) via the parameter-to-operator map. The family {μ_θ : θ ∈ Θ} is dense in 𝒫(𝒫) in the weak topology.

The amortised estimator Ψ_φ : Σ^L → Θ is trained to minimize the objective (13). Assuming that the true map G ↦ μ*_G is well approximated by some function in the hypothesis class of Ψ_φ, the universal approximation property of neural networks (or the genomic embedding network) ensures that the amortisation gap can be made arbitrarily small. More formally, for any ε > 0, there exists φ such that for all G in the support of the genome distribution,

W_2\big( \mu_{\Psi_\varphi(G)},\, \mu^*_G \big) \le \varepsilon.

During training, the empirical objective converges uniformly over G to the population objective, thanks to the Lipschitz continuity of the network and the bounded genome space. Thus the trained amortised estimator yields consistent operator measures.

