Appendix G. Wright–Fisher Stability of Operator Measures

G.1. Operator Fitness Functional

For a genome G, the phenotype-to-fitness map is φ : 𝒳 → ℝ. The fitness of G is w(G) = 𝔼_{x∼π_G}[φ(x)], the average fitness over the invariant measure of its developmental system. We assume w(G) is bounded and measurable.

The operator measure μ = Φ(G) is the distribution over operators for that genome. The fitness of an operator measure is defined as

\mathfrak{F}(\mu) = \int_{\Phi^{-1}(\mu)} w(G) \, \mathrm{d}\nu(G \mid \mu),

the conditional expectation of w(G) given that Φ(G) = μ, where ν is the population distribution over genomes. If Φ is not injective, this expectation weights all genomes mapping to μ by their frequencies.

G.2. Replicator Dynamics in Operator Space

Let ν_t be the probability measure on 𝒫(𝒫) describing the distribution of operator measures across the population at time t. The preimage of ν_t under Φ yields a frequency distribution over genomes. Under the infinite-population Wright–Fisher model with selection and mutation, the evolution of ν_t satisfies

\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \nu_t(\mu) = \nu_t(\mu) \big[ \mathfrak{F}(\mu) - \overline{\mathfrak{F}}_t \big] + \int \nu_t(\mu') \, m(\mu',\mu) \, \mathrm{d}\mu' - \nu_t(\mu) \, \overline{m}(\mu),

where m(μ',μ) is the mutation kernel from measure μ' to μ (obtained by integrating mutation over genomes in the equivalence class), and \overline{\mathfrak{F}}_t = ∫ 𝔉(μ) dν_t(μ). This is the replicator-mutator equation on the space of measures.

G.3. Evolutionary Stability Condition

A design pattern μ* is an evolutionarily stable operator measure if it is a locally asymptotically stable equilibrium of this dynamic. Linearizing around μ*:

\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \eta = \mathcal{L} \eta, \qquad \mathcal{L} = \text{diag}(\mathfrak{F}) - \langle \mathfrak{F}, \cdot \rangle \pi^* + \text{mutation operator},

where η is a signed measure perturbation. The stability requires that all eigenvalues of the operator ℒ in a suitable Banach space have negative real parts. Using the Otto calculus on the Wasserstein space, one can express the fitness gradient and Hessian to derive a Lyapunov functional.

In particular, when mutations are rare, the condition reduces to μ* being a local maximizer of 𝔉 under the constraint of fixed mutation neighborhood. This is the formal equivalent of the intuitive “local maximum of fitness” in Definition 1.5(ii). The thesis will characterize the Hessian of 𝔉 in terms of the second variation of the invariant measure π_G with respect to operator changes, using perturbation theory for Markov kernels.

